\f1\fs26 Shakti became a household name very quickly. His arrival at Public Affair Bureau coincided with the launch of Police Video Streaming. His face was on every Vid-Screen; Vid-board; poster; milk carton, leaflet and some smart-arse even put his face on toilet paper. Although paper products were prohibitively expensive, they sold like hot-cakes, mostly to police officers as it provided an opportunity to do something they had long wanted to do. There were two channels transmitted to levels 1 to 3 and one other channel for the consumption of Level 4 and above. The programming was almost entirely real life docu-dramas and with his flare for publicity and milking it was worth, his show took off. It was the first time in his life that he had useful and, in some cases, interesting ideas that took reality vid-streams to new lows - exactly what was required. Reality TV shows had long since proven that any idiot can be a star and Shakti, even with his advanced level idiocy, gained a level of acclaim.\
One program, Random Camera, revolved around surveillance and covert police operations, but not wrong the Public Affairs simpletons stomping around in their domain, Operational Police refused to assist with criminal intelligence. Starved of timely data, Shakti made a decision that only the truly unenlightened could make. He targeted houses at random. With this single decision, Shakti rejected all investigative theories and presumptions \'96 that potent crime suppression can only be effected by basing operations on timely and accurate intelligence. All senior police knew the show would be a failure as their collective experience brought them to a collective conclusion \'96 random policing yields random results. However, with a zero tolerance policy in effect, every show resulted in the unfortunate targets being detected committing some sort of criminal offence. \
Although the judiciary complained bitterly about the creation of an amateur parallel justice system, the amount of revenue raised ensured the program would survive. Clandestine video devices were installed into level 1 and 2 homes, at random, and the action was broadcast live into level 4-plus homes. The upper levels of society lapped it up. Most of them had never set foot on the ground. Living their lives above cloud height they had no idea how the common lived. \
Corporate Infidelity was, by and large, the major crime detected. This was not surprising, considering that to constitute the offence one merely had to breach employer confidentiality or defame a manager. A crime of absolute liability, the simple act of complaining to your spouse about work practices would establish this very serious offence. It struck at the very heart of the corporate structure and disrespect for managerial classes was inexcusable. It was the modern equivalent of heresy and doctrinal divergence of this magnitude was punishable by death and forfeiture all goods and chattels. Guilt and sentencing was decided by the viewers through on-line voting and carried out immediately. Very few in the audience were weak enough to feel compassion or deliver mercy to the accused lows. Most viewers correctly postulated that their voting habits were monitored and analysed for moral weakness \'96in this case compassion for one\'92s inferiors. Indulging one\'92s empathy was a waste of effort and interfered with the ability to make hard business decisions. Being outed for moral weakness had a monumentally negative effect on career paths and, consequently, there was hardly a show without at least one execution. Shows that did not feature the removal of someone\'92s breathing privileges rated rather poorly and advertising revenue for subsequent shows would plummet. When profits were threatened the corporate machine immediately rolled into action and issued a decree from on high \'96 One show, one body, no excuses. \
To upper society, this was the best show on the link. At 8.00pm every Tuesday and Thursday most, if not all, available level 5-pluses, were plonked in front of their Vid-screens or at level 5 cocktail bars, mesmerized by the outrageous and often bizarre behaviour of the lows. The subtitles at the bottom of the screen assisted them in understanding the gutter talk and expletives used incessantly by the common man. Many obtained no end of amusement in the lows use of syntax and the grotesque linguistic mutations that had developed below the layer. They especially loved it when they lows beat the shit out of each other \'96 domestic violence was hilarious, even to the female viewers. The level of violence was so extreme that it seemed the only thing commoners hated more than management, was each other.\
Shakti also had the balls, or the na\'efve stupidity, to suggest that a similar show be conceived for the consumption of the lows. He envisioned corporate fraudsters and rogue traders being brought to book live on his program. He would be an icon to the unwashed masses. The show went ahead, but Shakti was not made aware of some of the minor changes. All of the people featured on show were actors or impostors and no level 5\'92s were harmed during the production of any episode. Fueled by total ignorance, Shakti took to his new second show with a fervent enthusiasm. His genuine belief in the integrity of the program gave the fa\'e7ade a credibility that struck a chord with the discontented lows. At last someone was doing something about the morally bankrupt managers and the corporations they serve. \
In reality, anyone caught on the show was a convicted low already on a death sentence. They knew that the lows had an extraordinary and inexplicable attachment to family members and, like vultures, they pecked and tore at this devotion until leverage was found. To ensure they played along the production company promised social promotion and employment for their families. They were going to die anyway and figured that they may as well do some good for the kids before they got zapped. The final carrot dangled in front of the impostor was the possibility, as slim as it might be, that the audience of the lows might vote to spare him or her. Fat chance, they loved their payback. 
